# Redshift credentials for this dbt project.
#
# Copy to .env.redshift (gitignored), fill in, then:
#     ENV_FILE=.env.redshift make check-env             TARGET=redshift_prod
#     ENV_FILE=.env.redshift make verify-warehouse-smoke TARGET=redshift_prod
#     ENV_FILE=.env.redshift make verify-warehouse       TARGET=redshift_prod
#
# Why a file per warehouse: profiles.yml reads the same DB_TRANSFORM_USER and
# DB_TRANSFORM_PASSWORD for every target, so a single .env can only describe
# one warehouse at a time. Keeping .env.snowflake and .env.redshift side by
# side means testing one does not overwrite the other's credentials.
#
# dbt Core does NOT read .env files itself. The make targets load this file via
# scripts/with_env.py. A bare `dbt build` needs these exported in your shell,
# or use:
#     make dbt CMD="build --target redshift_prod"
#
# Values are parsed literally, not sourced through a shell, so a password
# containing \ $ ` or # survives intact. One layer of surrounding quotes is
# stripped if present.

# --- Cluster ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The endpoint HOSTNAME ONLY. profiles.yml supplies host, port and dbname
# separately, so do not include :5439 and do not append /database -- a full
# JDBC-style endpoint will fail to resolve.
#
#   right: my-cluster.abc123.eu-west-2.redshift.amazonaws.com
#   wrong: my-cluster.abc123.eu-west-2.redshift.amazonaws.com:5439/dev
REDSHIFT_HOST=XXX

# Optional. Omit entirely and profiles.yml defaults to 5439.
# REDSHIFT_PORT=5439

# --- Credentials -----------------------------------------------------------
DB_TRANSFORM_USER=etladmin

# Quote it: harmless, and it matters if the password contains a space or a #.
#
# Do not paste a JSON-escaped value. `terraform output` without -raw, Secrets
# Manager payloads and CloudFormation responses all escape special characters,
# and the literal text is then not the password -- it will be rejected as an
# authentication failure that points nowhere near the real cause. Use:
#     terraform output -raw <password_output_name>
DB_TRANSFORM_PASSWORD='XXX'

# --- Target ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Database name only. This user needs CREATE on it: the project creates four
# schemas -- extract, seeds, snapshots and transform -- because custom schemas
# are absolute rather than prefixed (macros/generate_schema_name.sql).
#
# Point it at a throwaway database. Because those schema names are fixed
# regardless of target, two targets sharing a database would overwrite each
# other's seeds and snapshot history.
DB_TRANSFORM_DATABASE=XXX

# --- Network ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The cluster must be publicly accessible and its security group must allow
# inbound TCP on the port above from your current IP. If `dbt debug` hangs
# rather than erroring, that is the security group; if it returns a
# password-authentication error, the connection itself is fine.
